"""Bundled lookup tables for the language + country layer (`litradar.i18n`).

Pure data, no logic. Everything here maps a phrase that occurs in author affiliation strings to an
ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code. Four kinds of entry:

* ``PHRASES`` - case-insensitive, accent-folded phrases matched on word boundaries: country names
  (English and native spellings, adjectives), first-level regions (Chinese provinces, US states,
  Canadian provinces, ...), major research cities and well-known institutions. Longest phrase wins
  and the matched span is consumed, so "Northern Ireland" -> GB never also yields IE, and
  "Chinese University of Hong Kong" -> HK never also yields CN. A value of ``None`` means
  "recognised but ambiguous: consume the span, assign nothing" (e.g. "China Medical University",
  which exists in both Taichung and Shenyang).
* ``ACRONYMS`` - case-sensitive tokens (``USA``, ``UK``, ``BGI``, ``MIT``, ``RIKEN`` ...).
* ``RAW_TERMS`` - substrings matched on the casefolded original text, for CJK and Cyrillic names
  that accent folding would erase (``中国科学院``, ``東京大学``, ``서울대학교``, ``москва``).
* ``ALPHA3`` - ISO 3166-1 alpha-3 codes, accepted only as the final comma-separated token of an
  affiliation ("..., Karaikal, IND"), the convention some journals use.
* ``US_STATE_CODES`` - two-letter US state codes, used only as a last-resort fallback when a string
  ends in one and nothing else matched ("..., Houston, TX").

Adding an entry: pick the most specific spelling, check it is not also a common word or a place
in a second country, and add a test in ``tests/test_i18n.py`` if it is a new pattern class.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

from typing import Dict, FrozenSet, Optional

